// docs/keybinds.kdl — paste the lines marked for copying into the `binds` node
// of your niri config (biri). File format and bind syntax are niri's; there is
// nothing here the compositor parses directly — copy the pieces, don't
// `include` this file.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WARNING — niri 26.04 LEAKS A PROCESS FOR EVERY FAILED `spawn`.
//
// Observed live in this session on 2026-09-29: a bare `spawn "nah"` bind
// ENOENT'd 1793 times (the client was not in niri's PATH) and leaked one
// Command-Spawner child process per failure. The leak grew to ≈ 12 GB RAM plus
// the full 8 GB swap and triggered a kernel OOM cascade that killed unrelated
// applications.
//
// `spawn` does NOT run a shell and does NOT search your shell's PATH; it execs
// the program directly in niri's environment. ALWAYS use an absolute path (as
// in every bind below), or verify the name resolves in niri's PATH:
//
//     tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/$(pgrep -x niri)/environ | grep '^PATH='
//
// (with a biri binary, `pgrep -x biri`). Never ship a bind that can ENOENT.
// Worth reporting upstream to niri: a failed spawn should not leak a child.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ONE chord set, screen-relative meaning, on every monitor:
//
//   Mod+Shift+H / L : "move the window one screen slot left / right"
//   Mod+Shift+K / J : "move the window one screen slot up / down"
//
// The `nah` daemon watches the focused output's transform and resolves the
// same chord to a different compositor action per orientation (tables locked
// and verified against the running niri 26.04 session — see
// docs/phase0-findings.md):
//
//   Horizontal output (transform 0 / 180 / flipped 0 / flipped 180):
//     move-left   -> ConsumeOrExpelWindowLeft
//     move-right  -> ConsumeOrExpelWindowRight
//     move-up     -> MoveWindowUp
//     move-down   -> MoveWindowDown
//
//   Vertical output (transform 90 / 270 / flipped 90 / flipped 270):
//     move-left   -> MoveColumnLeft
//     move-right  -> MoveColumnRight
//     move-up     -> ConsumeOrExpelWindowLeft
//     move-down   -> ConsumeOrExpelWindowRight
//
// A window travels across monitors with the same key.
//
//
// ### Start the daemon with the compositor
//
// Spawned processes inherit $NIRI_SOCKET, and a fresh one on every compositor
// restart, so no `systemctl --user import-environment` line is needed.
//
// Use the ABSOLUTE install path: bare `nah` works only while niri's own PATH
// resolves it, which is not the shell's PATH (see the warning above). The path
// below is the home-manager profile path (see "Packaging (Nix)" in
// README.md); before home-manager, the hand-installed copy lived at
// ~/.local/bin/nah. Adjust it if you installed elsewhere.
spawn-at-startup "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "--daemon"
//
// If you launch via systemd instead (docs/nah.service), DELETE this line.
// Running both launch paths at once makes the second instance hit the
// daemon's live-socket guard and exit non-zero.
//
//
// ### Binds
//
// These four lines REPLACE the old consume/expel/move binds once wired to
// these actions directly:
//   consume-or-expel-window-left / -right, move-window-up / -down
// (plus, on the rotated output, the old move-window-left / -right / whatever
// covers move-column-left / -right). Pick a free chord if any of these is
// already bound; on a conflict niri rejects the config on reload and shows a
// notification — pick a free chord.
//
// Absolute path in every bind: see the leak warning above.
Mod+Shift+H { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-left"; }
Mod+Shift+L { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-right"; }
Mod+Shift+K { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-up"; }
Mod+Shift+J { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-down"; }
//
// (The live config on this machine uses the same absolute path under
// Mod+Ctrl+H/L/K/J; keep the chord set ONE or the meanings overlap.)
//
// ### Key-repeat note
//
// `spawn` execs the client each press — fork/exec plus one socket round trip,
// a few milliseconds. Holding a chord for 5 s floods the daemon with retries,
// which is the intended volume; the daemon answers each with ok/err and no
// processes pile up (each client exits after its reply or ~100 ms timeout).
//
//
// ### Alternative chords (uncomment, or swap letters to taste)
//
// Arrow-style:
//
// Mod+Left  { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-left"; }
// Mod+Right { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-right"; }
// Mod+Up    { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-up"; }
// Mod+Down  { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-down"; }
//
// no-modifier-shift style — beware: bare-letter binds swallow the key in the
// compositor, breaking texting into other apps on that chord:
//
// Mod+H { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-left"; }
// Mod+L { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-right"; }
// Mod+K { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-up"; }
// Mod+J { spawn "/home/fbaumgardt/.nix-profile/bin/nah" "move-down"; }
//
// Keep the chord set ONE; the whole point is that its meaning follows the
// focused monitor's orientation, not the letter.
